# Insoora marketing site — environment
#
# Copy to `.env.local` for development. On Vercel, set these under
# Project → Settings → Environment Variables, for BOTH Production and
# Preview. Both are NEXT_PUBLIC_ and therefore inlined at BUILD time:
# changing either needs a redeploy, not a restart.

# ── Where the product lives ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE ONE VALUE THAT REALLY HAS TO BE SET. Every "Sign in" and "Get Started"
# control points into this origin, and /api/enquiries relays the contact form
# to it server-side.
#
# Unset in production, the build does NOT fail — it logs a warning and falls
# back to the value below, which is this project's intended host as recorded
# here. It never falls back to localhost: when it did, the deployed site
# served `http://localhost:3000/agent/register` on every primary button.
#
# Confirm the host below actually resolves and serves the product. At the time
# of writing it does not, because the domain is not connected yet — so until
# it is, set this to wherever the product is really reachable, or accept that
# the conversion path leads somewhere that is not answering yet. Check the
# deploy log for the warning.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://app.insoora.com

# ── This site's own public origin ────────────────────────────────────────
# OPTIONAL, AND THE INDEXING SWITCH.
#
# Canonical URLs, Open Graph URLs, sitemap.xml and robots.txt are built from
# it. Leave it UNSET until the real domain is actually connected and serving:
#
#   unset  → canonicals fall back to the deployment's own Vercel production
#            host (never localhost; off Vercel, to the domain below), and
#            every page is noindex, robots.txt disallows everything and the
#            sitemap is empty. That stops the *.vercel.app copy being indexed
#            and then having to be de-indexed once the real domain goes live.
#   set    → that domain becomes the canonical origin and the whole site
#            becomes indexable. Only set it when the domain resolves.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://www.insoora.com

# ── Legal review callouts ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The Privacy Policy and Terms pages are drafts, and the sections still
# needing a real fact or a legal decision render as visible "Needs review"
# callouts. Set this to `off` ONLY once that review is actually done — it is
# a deliberate act by whoever completed it, not a build-time tidy-up.
# LEGAL_REVIEW_FLAGS=off

# ── Analytics and the cookie consent gate ────────────────────────────────
# No variable to set. Recorded here because the behaviour surprises people.
#
# Google Analytics (G-C683RQCP76) and Tag Manager (GTM-5943B5QG) are compiled
# into the site but load ONLY when a visitor accepts analytics cookies, and
# ONLY when NODE_ENV=production. So:
#
#   * `next dev`  → no tags, and no consent banner either (a banner asking
#                   permission for cookies that cannot be set would be a lie).
#   * production  → the banner appears, and nothing from googletagmanager.com
#                   is requested until someone clicks Accept.
#
# To see the gate working locally, build and start rather than running dev:
#   npm run build && npm start
#
# The choice lives in the visitor's own browser (localStorage key
# `insoora.analytics-consent`). It never reaches the server, so it is NOT a
# DPDP consent record — see the note at the top of src/lib/consent.ts.

# ── Contact form email notifications ─────────────────────────────────────
# Added when the business asked for an email on every contact-form
# submission. Before this, an enquiry became a row in the product's database
# and nothing else — a notification reaching anybody depended on somebody
# opening the back office.
#
# The form posts to /api/enquiries on THIS host, which now does two things
# with one submission: relays it to the product (the system of record) and
# emails it to the address below. Those two sinks are independent on purpose
# — an enquiry is reported as received if either accepted it, and only shown
# as failed if both did. See src/app/api/enquiries/route.ts.
#
# NONE of these is NEXT_PUBLIC_, and none may become so. They are read only
# in the route handler and in src/lib/email, which the browser never loads.

# The Resend API key. UNSET IS A SUPPORTED STATE, and it is not an error:
# with no key the form still works, the enquiry is still stored by the
# product, and the route logs `notified=not-configured`. What does NOT happen
# is any email, and the success panel then correctly says nothing about one.
#
# Get it from https://resend.com → API Keys. A sending key ("Sending access")
# is enough; it does not need full access.
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# Who the notification is addressed to. Comma-separated for more than one.
#
# Defaults to toyuvrajraulji@gmail.com, which is the address the business
# confirmed. Note for anyone reading the original brief: it asked for
# `toyuvrraulji@gmail.com`, which differs by two characters and was confirmed
# to be a typo. Do not "correct" the default back to it.
# ENQUIRY_NOTIFY_EMAIL=toyuvrajraulji@gmail.com

# The From address. MUST be on a domain verified in the Resend dashboard, or
# every send is rejected with a 403 saying so — this is the single most likely
# reason for email to be configured and still not arrive.
#
# Defaults to `Insoora Website <enquiries@insoora.com>`. Two separate things
# have to be true for that default to work:
#
#   1. insoora.com is verified in Resend, with its SPF, DKIM and DMARC records
#      published in DNS. Without this, nothing sends.
#   2. Someone reads mail arriving at enquiries@insoora.com. Nothing breaks if
#      nobody does — the notification sets Reply-To to the enquirer, so the
#      business replying works either way, and the visitor's confirmation
#      deliberately does not invite a reply. But if that mailbox IS monitored,
#      the confirmation can be reworded to invite one; see the note on
#      `visitorConfirmation` in src/lib/email/enquiry-emails.ts.
# ENQUIRY_FROM_EMAIL=Insoora Website <enquiries@insoora.com>

# ── On rate limiting ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No variable to set. Recorded because the limitation is easy to miss.
#
# The route rate-limits per IP (5 submissions per 10 minutes) and collapses an
# accidental double-submit, using a Map in module scope. On a serverless
# platform that is PER INSTANCE: concurrent lambdas have separate counters and
# a cold start has an empty one. It stops a double-click and a single script
# from one address, which is what actually happens to a contact form; it is
# not a defence against a distributed flood, and nothing claims it is.
#
# The product's own /api/enquiries rate-limits per IP as well, durably, and
# this site forwards the visitor's address so that limit sees the visitor
# rather than this server. If abuse ever becomes real rather than theoretical,
# the upgrade is a KV or Redis store behind the same two functions in the
# route — nothing else has to change.
